`checkRootFilesUpdate` replaces `this.commandLine.fileNames` with the freshly parsed list from `getCommandLineFn()`, which contains only the files the tsconfig statically includes. Any story files that were dynamically injected via `ensureFiles()` are silently dropped. On the very next TS program build after any file is created or deleted (which sets `shouldCheckRootFiles = true`), the language service loses all story files. Although the warmup timer re-adds them 100 ms later, any extraction request arriving in that window will miss every story-file based type resolution (Path 1 fails; Path 2 may degrade too).

Fix: track dynamically added files in a separate `private extraFiles = new Set<string>()` set inside `ensureFiles`, then merge them back when rebuilding the file list:
```ts
if (!arrayItemsEqual(newCommandLine.fileNames, base)) {
  this.commandLine.fileNames = [
    ...newCommandLine.fileNames,
    ...this.extraFiles,
  ];
  this.projectVersion++;
}
```

Empty `catch {}` silently discards any error thrown during the background warmup. Per the project's logging conventions, at minimum log at debug level:
```ts
} catch (err) {
  logger.debug(`[reactComponentMeta] Warmup extraction failed: ${err}`);
}
```

When `componentName` is provided, the new code does a hard `return` on the result of `components.find((it) => it.componentName === componentName)`. This returns `undefined` immediately when the exact match fails—there is no fall-through to title-based matching. The original code also checked `it.localImportName` and `it.importName`, which handled the edge case where `meta.component` references the original export name of an aliased import (e.g., `import { ButtonBase as Button }` with `meta.component = 'ButtonBase'` in the meta object). The new logic silently skips such stories instead of falling back. Consider soft-returning:
```ts
if (componentName) {
  const match = components.find((it) => it.componentName === componentName);
  if (match) return match;
}
// fall through to title-based matching
```
